1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:06,395
This poster is titled Tick-Primed Hypersensitivity in Cosmetic and Aesthetic Procedures.

2
00:00:06,345 --> 00:00:15,978
The authors are Marielle He, M-D, M-S; Jordan Kai Simmons, M-D; and Babak Azizzadeh, M-D, F-A-C-S.

3
00:00:15,978 --> 00:00:29,279
They are affiliated with the CENTER for Advanced Facial Plastic Surgery in Beverly Hills, California, and the Division of Otolaryngology–Head and Neck Surgery at Cedars-Sinai Medical Center in Los Angeles.

4
00:00:29,279 --> 00:00:32,934
Section one: Alpha-gal syndrome overview.

5
00:00:32,934 --> 00:00:38,519
The central concept is that being sensitized is not the same as being reactive.

6
00:00:38,519 --> 00:00:50,842
Alpha-gal syndrome, abbreviated A-G-S, requires both a compatible clinical history and alpha-gal–specific I-G-E. Elevated I-G-E alone does not establish the diagnosis.

7
00:00:50,842 --> 00:01:03,505
What it is: Alpha-gal syndrome is an I-G-E–mediated allergy to galactose-alpha-1,3-galactose, called alpha-gal, a sugar found in non-primate mammalian tissues and products.

8
00:01:03,505 --> 00:01:20,054
Why it matters: In cosmetic and aesthetic practice, this creates a potential anaphylaxis risk from gelatin hemostats, collagen-containing fillers, mammalian grafts, catgut sutures, hyaluronidase, and various excipients.

9
00:01:20,054 --> 00:01:32,622
Epidemiology: According to C-D-C Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report data from 2017 to 2022, there were about 90,018 suspected cases.

10
00:01:32,622 --> 00:01:39,891
An estimated 96,000 to 450,000 people have been affected since 2010.

11
00:01:39,891 --> 00:01:52,432
Importantly, being seropositive does not equal having disease—for example, seropositivity reached 31.2 percent in Arkansas and 26 percent in Missouri.

12
00:01:52,432 --> 00:01:57,921
Geographically, cases cluster in the southern and eastern United States.

13
00:01:57,921 --> 00:02:13,342
Review design: The authors reviewed literature published from January 2010 through July 2026, with a final search on August 2, 2026, prioritizing surgical and perioperative alpha-gal data.

14
00:02:13,342 --> 00:02:25,407
Limits: There is no prospective cosmetic or aesthetic cohort, no reaction registry, and no validated product-specific I-G-E threshold or brand-level alpha-gal assay.

15
00:02:25,407 --> 00:02:28,980
Section two: Clinical decision-making.

16
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:33,369
The framework is Screen, then Stratify, then Act.

17
00:02:33,369 --> 00:02:39,388
Under Screen: Step one, take a history of prior reactions and tick exposure.

18
00:02:39,388 --> 00:02:51,711
Step two, establish the diagnosis once, based on a compatible history plus alpha-gal I-G-E. Confirmation defines the diagnosis; it is not a per-procedure laboratory gate.

19
00:02:51,711 --> 00:03:02,336
Under Stratify: Step three, audit all materials—hemostats, fillers, grafts, sutures, hyaluronidase, and excipients.

20
00:03:02,336 --> 00:03:08,559
Step four, verify the species, source, excipients, and lot number.

21
00:03:08,559 --> 00:03:16,059
Under Act: Step five, substitute with source-verified hyaluronic acid and non-gelatin options.

22
00:03:16,059 --> 00:03:23,722
Step six, prepare with epinephrine and a reaction plan, and consult allergy if the situation is uncertain.

23
00:03:23,722 --> 00:03:27,635
Three key clinical caveats accompany this framework.

24
00:03:27,635 --> 00:03:31,739
First, food data does not equal product cutoffs.

25
00:03:31,739 --> 00:03:54,605
Reference thresholds—such as 2.0 kilounits per liter or 0.75 percent total I-G-E giving greater than 95 percent positive predictive value, and greater than 5.5 kilounits per liter or 2.12 percent giving roughly 95 percent modeled probability—are not validated for cosmetic products.

26
00:03:54,605 --> 00:03:57,595
Second, route changes timing.

27
00:03:57,595 --> 00:04:02,527
Oral exposure causes delayed reactions at three to six hours.

28
00:04:02,527 --> 00:04:05,624
Parenteral exposure may be immediate.

29
00:04:05,624 --> 00:04:08,641
Implants cause prolonged exposure.

30
00:04:08,641 --> 00:04:12,282
Parenteral delivery bypasses gut processing.

31
00:04:12,282 --> 00:04:15,149
Third, source and lot matter.

32
00:04:15,149 --> 00:04:23,152
Verify species, source, excipients, and lot, because formulations change and labels may be incomplete.

33
00:04:23,152 --> 00:04:27,717
Section three: Material risk, graded by evidence.

34
00:04:27,717 --> 00:04:35,815
The recurring principle: sensitized is not reactive—history plus alpha-gal I-G-E define A-G-S risk.

35
00:04:35,815 --> 00:04:47,921
Evidence is graded from strongest to weakest as follows: documented reaction, then clinical signal, then mechanism, then theory, then no A-G-S-specific literature.

36
00:04:47,921 --> 00:04:53,994
Gelatin hemostats, such as Gelfoam and Floseal, have demonstrated evidence.

37
00:04:53,994 --> 00:04:59,891
There is class-level perioperative anaphylaxis, though not every brand is implicated.

38
00:04:59,891 --> 00:05:06,793
Porcine unfractionated heparin, at cardiac-dose intravenous levels, shows a clinical signal.

39
00:05:06,793 --> 00:05:14,130
High-dose intravenous cardiac exposure: 4 of 17 severe reactions, or 24 percent.

40
00:05:14,130 --> 00:05:16,331
Route and dose matter.

41
00:05:16,331 --> 00:05:20,081
Heparin is not an absolute contraindication.

42
00:05:20,236 --> 00:05:26,300
Mammalian matrices, such as Strattice and SurgiMend, have mechanistic evidence.

43
00:05:26,250 --> 00:05:31,644
There is surgical precedent but no prospective cosmetic or aesthetic cohort.

44
00:05:31,644 --> 00:05:39,402
Collagen, P-M-M-A, and catgut—for example Bellafill and surgical gut—are rated theoretical.

45
00:05:39,402 --> 00:05:44,538
Concern is composition-based, with no A-G-S reaction series.

46
00:05:44,538 --> 00:05:51,222
Animal hyaluronidase, such as Amphadase and Vitrase, is theoretical for alpha-gal only.

47
00:05:51,222 --> 00:05:54,130
These are testicular extracts.

48
00:05:54,130 --> 00:06:00,543
Recombinant human hyaluronidase—Hylenex—is the non-animal-extract alternative.

49
00:06:00,543 --> 00:06:08,097
Animal-derived products also carry an independent, label-documented, non-A-G-S anaphylaxis risk.

50
00:06:08,097 --> 00:06:11,834
Theoretical does not equal low overall risk.

51
00:06:11,834 --> 00:06:27,649
Finally, source-verified substitutes—hyaluronic acid, calcium hydroxylapatite, poly-L-lactic acid, and lidocaine—have no A-G-S-specific literature, though other hypersensitivity mechanisms remain possible.

52
00:06:27,649 --> 00:06:32,527
Absence of A-G-S-specific literature does not equal proven safe.

53
00:06:32,527 --> 00:06:34,769
Three closing notes.

54
00:06:34,769 --> 00:06:42,839
On hyaluronidase: recombinant sourcing removes only the glycan variable; protein-directed allergy remains.

55
00:06:42,839 --> 00:06:52,676
On neutrality: brands are cited to illustrate material classes—this is not endorsement, and no brand-level alpha-gal assays were performed.

56
00:06:52,676 --> 00:07:05,720
On currency: examples are current as of August 2026; always verify the current F-D-A label and instructions for use, plus species, source, excipients, and lot.

57
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:17,649
The take-home message: In alpha-gal syndrome, sensitization does not equal reactivity—diagnosis requires both a compatible history and alpha-gal-specific I-G-E.

58
00:07:17,649 --> 00:07:35,978
For cosmetic and aesthetic procedures, screen for history and tick exposure, stratify by auditing and verifying all mammalian-derived materials, and act by substituting source-verified non-gelatin, non-animal options while preparing for anaphylaxis when risk is uncertain.

59
00:07:35,978 --> 00:07:47,894
A digital companion with full citations, accessible text, and audio is available at the Q-R code, at tinyurl.com/AGS-FPRS.

60
00:07:47,894 --> 00:07:50,869
This concludes the poster narration.
